# Finder

A finder searches one defined collection and uses relevant filters to narrow its results. Say what the collection is before the person types, because a search that quietly covers more or less than expected gives wrong answers with full confidence.

This is a reference pattern. The examples are simulated with invented records. They do not search Home Assistant, connect a backend or add a capability.

<h2 id="finder-shared-contract">Shared finder behaviour</h2>

Every finder variant follows these rules. The variant pages link here instead of repeating them.

- **Scope.** Put the collection next to the search label, for example “Search the example collection”. Do not mix result types without labelling each one (WG-001).
- **Results.** Give each result a stable title and only the metadata needed to tell it apart. Show the result count and the criteria in use.
- **Empty states.** Show a loading state while results are pending. A no-match result says so and offers a way to broaden the query. An unavailable source gets a different message, because “nothing found” and “could not search” lead to different next steps (WG-033, WG-038).
- **Filters.** Let people remove one filter without re-entering the whole query. Keep the criteria when they return from a result.
- **Focus and access.** Keep keyboard focus predictable as results change, and announce the count through a status message.
- **Without JavaScript.** Show every record and state that the form cannot submit.
- **Permissions.** Filter by permission before data is exposed.

Use the [search filter pattern](../patterns/search-filter.html) for query, facet, count and reset behaviour and the [search input component](../../components/search-input.html) for the field itself. This page owns the collection composition.

The simulated examples show the scope, count, no-match state, status announcement and no-JavaScript fallback. Showing the criteria in use, removing a single filter, returning to results with the criteria kept, and the unavailable state are requirements for a real implementation that the examples do not show.

<h2 id="finder-variants">Retained source contexts</h2>

The eleven variants below come from the government publishing guide. Each page applies the shared behaviour to a household collection. Where a government directory has no household equivalent, the page says so rather than inventing a service.

| Variant | Household boundary |
| --- | --- |
| [Case studies](finder/case-studies-finder.html) | Curated project evidence. Label synthetic examples and tested records accurately. |
| [Organisations](finder/departments-agencies-public-bodies-finder.html) | Integration or provider context only. No invented government affiliation. |
| [Groups](finder/groups-finder.html) | Explicitly configured groups. A matching name does not prove membership. |
| [Contacts](finder/hmrc-contacts-finder.html) | Verified support destinations. No invented contact details or response times. |
| [People](finder/people-finder.html) | Private roles only. Account permissions and presence privacy stay separate. |
| [Search](finder/search.html) | A stated documentation scope, kept apart from private household data. |
| [Specialist documents](finder/specialist-document-finder.html) | Model and revision context. A name match does not establish compatibility. |
| [Data sets](finder/statistical-data-sets-finder.html) | Real measurements or existing exports. No imaginary download endpoint. |
| [Broad purposes](finder/supergroup-finder.html) | Predictable guidance categories that do not replace native area or domain grouping. |
| [Projects and events](finder/topical-events-finder.html) | Current and archived projects told apart by real status and dates. |
| [Worldwide organisations](finder/world-organisation-finder.html) | No household equivalent. Multi-home discovery is not implemented. |

Extend a collection only when its records, permissions and destinations exist. Do not infer live discovery or account management from a directory layout.

## When to use it

Use a finder for a device, automation, guide or maintenance-record collection that is large enough to need searching. Say whether the search covers entity names, friendly names, model numbers or document text. Home Assistant’s own search keeps owning entity and action discovery. A documentation finder must not look like a search of the home.

